## Why This Is Asked
Tests 128-bit rotation across word boundary without per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value as two 64-bit words; cross-word mixing; edge k<64 vs k>=64

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi, lo; } uint128_t;
static inline uint128_t rotl128(uint128_t x, unsigned k){
  k %= 128; uint128_t r;
  if (k < 64){
    r.hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    r.lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
  } else { unsigned kk = k - 64;
    r.hi = (x.lo << kk) | (x.hi >> (64 - kk));
    r.lo = (x.hi << kk) | (x.lo >> (64 - kk));
  }
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test boundary cases k=0, 63, 64, 127?
- Could you adapt to 256-bit using 4x uint64_t without loops?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests extending known 64-bit tricks to 128-bit values with branchless code.

## Key Concepts
- Branchless bit counting
- 64-bit popcount kernel
- Combining results across two words

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x -= (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint8_t popcount128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo){
  return (uint8_t)(popcount64(hi) + popcount64(lo));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for SIMD/vectorized paths?
- How would you test correctness across random 128-bit inputs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply SWAR-style bit counting across a 128-bit structure without branches, using two independent 64-bit halves. Also checks portability when no native 128-bit constant exists.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR popcount on 64-bit chunks
- 128-bit represented as { hi, lo }
- Edge-case handling for 0 and all-ones

## Code Example

```c
uint8_t popcount128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo = lo - ((lo >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  lo = (lo & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((lo >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  lo = (lo + (lo >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  lo = lo + (lo >> 8);
  lo = lo + (lo >> 16);
  lo = lo + (lo >> 32);

  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi = hi - ((hi >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  hi = (hi & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((hi >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  hi = (hi + (hi >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  hi = hi + (hi >> 8);
  hi = hi + (hi >> 16);
  hi = hi + (hi >> 32);

  uint8_t cl = lo & 0x7F;
  uint8_t ch = hi & 0x7F;
  return cl + ch;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for wider word sizes (e.g., 256-bit)?
- Could compiler intrinsics offer portable fallbacks here?

